WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.369
Swedish historian Johan Norberg

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.319
is the author of The Capitalist

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.209
Manifesto: Why the Global

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.129
Free Market Will Save the World,

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.199
which caught the eye of Elon Musk,

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.089
who recently tweeted: "this book,

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.009
is an excellent explanation of why

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:16.349
capitalism is not just successful

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.359
but morally right." Norberg

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.069
wrote the book to combat a growing

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.079
belief on the right and the left

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.789
that libertarian values of

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.799
individual autonomy, property

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.779
rights, limited government and free

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.609
enterprise are failing

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.649
to raise living standards and need

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.269
to be ditched in favor of more

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.339
centralized power and control

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.259
over virtually all aspects

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:38.099
of our lives.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.539
A senior fellow at the Cato

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.759
Institute, Norberg, shows that life

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.469
is actually getting better for all

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.899
of us, especially the world's poor,

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.939
and that economic globalization,

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.619
political liberalization and

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.659
cultural freedom are the main

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.130
drivers of improvement.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:00:59.909
Johan Norberg.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:01.319
Thanks for talking to Reason.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:02.320
Pleasure's all mine.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:04.649
First off, let's start.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.749
What's the elevator pitch?

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.159
Or do Swedes call it the

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:10.619
lift  in English? What do you go

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.479
with? Is it a lift or an elevator?

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.919
We call it a hiss.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:14.939
A hiss. Okay.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:15.749
What is it?

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:18.179
What is the hiss pitch?

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.009
You know, for the capitalist

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.089
manifesto?

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.069
Well, the his pitch is that,

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.609
you know, we have wide discontent

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.619
with capitalism and free

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:29.969
markets and free trade.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.099
It seems like politicians

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:33.869
on all continents have all agreed

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.489
that we need to repatriate

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.529
production, redistribute

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.569
resources, that inequality

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:41.639
and global warming shows us that

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.139
that free markets doesn't work.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.539
And my point is that on

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.419
each of us in every area,

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:49.929
free markets, entrepreneurs,

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:51.989
innovators have performed

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.119
spectacularly well

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.249
and that most of these things

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:57.989
have been misunderstood.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:01:59.849
And even in the 21st

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:01.709
century, I mean,

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:03.899
the past 20 years or

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.459
so have been pretty good, even

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.499
though there's a lot of people that

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.388
you and I have kind of been allies

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:11.579
with ideologically over the years

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:12.869
who are like, Oh yeah, yeah, it was

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.819
great up until 2000 and now

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.409
it's all gone to shit.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.239
Let's start by talking about yeah,

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:19.949
it was like about 20 years ago that

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.869
you and I, I think first talked and

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.699
you were pushing a book called

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.710
In Defense of Global Capitalism.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:28.679
Kind of sketch out what were

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.809
the circumstances that book

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.419
needed to be manifested.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.279
Well, they were the same as they

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:36.209
are today.

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.129
Politicians and interest

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:40.139
groups blamed global

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:41.729
capitalism and free trade for all

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:43.439
the problems of the world.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.449
Back then, a leftist

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:47.710
current of

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.959
anti-globalization and

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.119
reducing free trade

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.199
around the world.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.059
And I thought I had to gather

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:58.919
the argument because I thought that

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.449
was I was seeing around the world

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.219
was that the very countries that

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.869
began to integrate into the global

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:05.759
economy, they did better than they

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:06.929
had ever done before.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.759
Poverty was decreasing,

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.459
opportunities were improving

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.649
and cutting

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:15.779
them off from globalization destroy

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:15.929
that.

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:17.579
Yeah. What were the big arguments

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.469
emanating? And I mean, it was

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.299
I guess it was center

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.339
and center from

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:24.869
the center going towards the left,

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.489
especially the right wing seemed to

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:28.589
be, at least in America, was

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:30.449
broadly supportive of globalization

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:31.859
and trade. And, you know, George

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.869
H.W. Bush had started

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:34.919
NAFTA.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.659
Bill Clinton, who was a centrist

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:38.399
Democrat, you know, finished the job

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:39.419
and everything. But, you know, we

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:40.949
were high off of winning the Cold

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:41.759
War.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:43.109
But by the end of the decade, you

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.269
had people dressing like sea turtles

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:46.799
and smashing Starbucks and

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.659
McDonald's in Seattle.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.579
What was the main complaint

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.069
coming from the anti-globalization

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:54.070
then?

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.159
Well, it wasn't portrayed as a

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:57.449
justice movement.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:58.859
The very idea that, yes,

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.779
multinational companies

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:02.789
and big brands and free trade,

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:04.709
they might benefit us in the rich

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.059
world, but it will come at a

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.039
terrible cost in poor countries

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.649
exploitation, widening inequalities,

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.209
increasing poverty.

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.759
So basically a zero sum idea

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.199
about how the world works.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:18.328
If we benefit, they will lose.

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:20.159
And ironically, I

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.109
get the same thing today, but

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:23.579
the positions have been reversed.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.439
Okay. Yeah, let's talk about that

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:26.999
because yeah, I mean, you flash

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.979
forward 20, 20 years

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.319
or so and it seems like it's more

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:32.969
right wingers are now saying, you

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:34.919
know what, we need to nail

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:35.759
down the borders.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:37.769
We need to build more walls.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:39.869
Globalization benefits

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:41.309
these global, you know,

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:43.499
corporations, not the little guy.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.449
Quite right. And actually,

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.279
they sound very much like

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:49.079
'attack the 20 years ago, this

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.029
fringe left wing protectionist

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:52.889
organization.' But now from a

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:55.079
right wing nationalist

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.059
perspective and in a way, they have

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:59.009
the same idea that the world

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:00.209
is a zero sum game.

